# pdf.xyz storage-artifact cleanup evidence check — cron EXAMPLE (Phase 43).
# Copy to /etc/cron.d/pdfxyz-cleanup-check.
#
# Runs the inert notifier wrapper hourly: it PROJECTS a raw provider export you captured
# out-of-band into the content-free Phase 42 shapes and runs the absence verifier + dry-run
# sweeper, mapping their documented exit codes to an action. It makes NO cloud call and
# DELETES NOTHING — object deletion is operator-owned and out-of-band. This is a TEMPLATE.
#
# Exit ladder rolled up by the wrapper: 0 ok · 2 unknown · 3 drift · 4 breach. Usage(1) from
# a misconfigured check is folded to unknown(2), never a silent success. The wrapper re-raises
# 2/3/4 so cron's MAILTO mails you on a genuine cleanup gap.
#
# `/etc/cron.d` rows carry a user field (run as an unprivileged operator, never root). For a
# per-user crontab (`crontab -e`) drop the `pdfxyz` user column.
#
# Point the wrapper at your out-of-band export and mail non-empty output to your operator
# address (a real MTA/relay is out-of-band):
MAILTO=REPLACE-ME-operator@example.invalid
PDFXYZ_CLEANUP_RAW_EXPORT=/var/lib/pdfxyz/cleanup/raw-export.json

# m h dom mon dow  user    command
17 * * * *         pdfxyz  /opt/pdfxyz/deploy/cleanup/cleanup-evidence-notify.sh

# Prefer to inline it instead of the wrapper? Then encode the SAME semantics yourself — alert
# on exit >= 2, and treat 1 as "the check itself is broken", e.g.:
# 17 * * * * pdfxyz cd /opt/pdfxyz && pnpm -s worker:cleanup-project --target=evidence --file="$PDFXYZ_CLEANUP_RAW_EXPORT" | pnpm -s worker:cleanup-check --stdin --json; c=$?; [ "$c" -ge 2 ] && your-notifier "cleanup-check exit $c"
